# Section D — PostgreSQL planner

## Indexed candidate lookup

```sql
EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)
SELECT notice_id
FROM lsh_buckets
WHERE band_no = 0
  AND bucket_key = (
      SELECT bucket_key
      FROM lsh_buckets
      WHERE band_no = 0
      LIMIT 1
  );
```

Measured plan:

```text
Index Scan using idx_lsh_bucket_lookup on lsh_buckets

Planning Time: 0.058 ms
Execution Time: 0.063 ms
Buffers: shared hit=40
```

Index:

```sql
CREATE INDEX idx_lsh_bucket_lookup
ON lsh_buckets (band_no, bucket_key);
```

---

# Section E — Bucket work distribution

```sql
SELECT
    band_no,
    bucket_key,
    COUNT(*) AS bucket_size
FROM lsh_buckets
GROUP BY band_no, bucket_key
ORDER BY bucket_size DESC;
```

Measured distribution:

```text
Buckets: 53289
Maximum bucket size: 3367
Average bucket size: 4.5
Median bucket size: 1
95th percentile: 10
99th percentile: 55
```

## Mitigation

Rule:

```text
Ignore LSH buckets containing more than 100 notices.
```

Measured label effect:

Before:
```text
SAME: 204 / 279 = 73.12%
DIFFERENT: 250 / 621 = 40.26%
```

After:
```text
SAME: 164 / 279 = 58.78%
DIFFERENT: 28 / 621 = 4.51%
```

Large buckets affected:
`282`

Interpretation:
The mitigation reduces large candidate lists and sharply reduces DIFFERENT-pair survival, but it costs SAME-pair recall. The quality cost must therefore be reported.

---

# Useful viva queries

## Show table sizes

```sql
SELECT 'notices' AS table_name, COUNT(*) FROM notices
UNION ALL
SELECT 'labelled_pairs', COUNT(*) FROM labelled_pairs
UNION ALL
SELECT 'notice_signatures', COUNT(*) FROM notice_signatures
UNION ALL
SELECT 'lsh_buckets', COUNT(*) FROM lsh_buckets;
```

## Show indexes

```sql
SELECT tablename, indexname, indexdef
FROM pg_indexes
WHERE tablename = 'lsh_buckets';
```

## Show settings

```sql
SELECT *
FROM settings
ORDER BY key;
```

Expected settings include:

```text
false_merge_cost_ratio = 10
retrieval_target_recall = 0.95
```

## Inspect an opportunity mapping

```sql
SELECT no.notice_id, no.opportunity_id
FROM notice_opportunity no
ORDER BY no.opportunity_id, no.notice_id
LIMIT 20;
```

## Count notices mapped to opportunities

```sql
SELECT opportunity_id, COUNT(*) AS notice_count
FROM notice_opportunity
GROUP BY opportunity_id
ORDER BY notice_count DESC;
```
